WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.028
A monitoring baseline is a reference for expected assets, communication paths, protocols,

00:00:05.028 --> 00:00:07.081
roles, timing, and operational behavior.

00:00:07.081 --> 00:00:09.545
OT networks often have repeatable patterns,

00:00:09.545 --> 00:00:14.473
which makes meaningful changes visible when the baseline is accurate and maintained.

00:00:14.473 --> 00:00:16.527
Start with inventory and architecture.

00:00:16.527 --> 00:00:20.633
Identify expected devices, their function, normal peers, approved remote connections,

00:00:20.633 --> 00:00:22.687
engineering pathways, and trust boundaries.

00:00:22.687 --> 00:00:26.793
Observe several operating conditions, including startup, shutdown, maintenance, batch changes,

00:00:26.793 --> 00:00:29.257
failover, and seasonal or production variation.

00:00:29.257 --> 00:00:34.596
Anomalies may include a new device, a new communication pair, an unexpected protocol,

00:00:34.596 --> 00:00:39.113
activity outside an approved window, changed message frequency, unusual engineering access,

00:00:39.113 --> 00:00:43.220
or a command pattern that differs from the normal role.

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:48.559
A change in behavior is evidence for investigation, not proof of malicious activity.

00:00:48.559 --> 00:00:50.612
Process faults, equipment replacement, troubleshooting,

00:00:50.612 --> 00:00:53.487
and authorized maintenance can all create anomalies.

00:00:53.487 --> 00:00:59.647
Operators and engineers can explain whether the observed activity matches the process and work plan.

00:00:59.647 --> 00:01:06.217
Monitoring teams should capture that context so the same approved pattern does not create repeated noise.

00:01:06.217 --> 00:01:12.377
Baselines can also preserve unsafe behavior if the learning period contains unmanaged access or misconfiguration.

00:01:12.377 --> 00:01:15.663
Compare observed patterns with approved architecture and policy.

00:01:15.663 --> 00:01:18.537
Known normal is not automatically known good.

00:01:18.537 --> 00:01:20.591
Update baselines through change control.

00:01:20.591 --> 00:01:25.108
Document new equipment, firmware, production paths, vendor access, and expected timing.

00:01:25.108 --> 00:01:26.751
Tune detection using evidence,

00:01:26.751 --> 00:01:30.857
but avoid suppressing entire categories simply because they are noisy.

00:01:30.857 --> 00:01:34.143
Effective anomaly detection balances sensitivity with operational understanding,

00:01:34.143 --> 00:01:38.660
producing a manageable set of changes that knowledgeable teams can investigate.
